
  Consequence worth stating plainly: after this, [1mopening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working[0m — module scripts
  are blocked on [38;5;153mfile://[39m. You'll need [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar. That's inherent to the ES-modules decision, not
  something the design adds.

  [1mSection 3 — Testing, and one wrinkle I hit[0m

  The repo has [1mno test runner, no linter, no build step, no CI[0m. It also has a module-system conflict I need to flag:

  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so Node treats [38;5;153m.js[39m as CommonJS — which is what [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m rely
  on. But [38;5;153msrc/settings.js[39m must be ESM for the browser. The browser ignores [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m entirely, so [1mthe app works fine
[0m  [1meither way[0m; the conflict only bites if you want to run Node tests against [38;5;153msrc/settings.js[39m. Resolving it means either
  adding [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m and renaming the two CommonJS files to [38;5;153m.cjs[39m, or naming the new file [38;5;153msrc/settings.mjs[39m
  (unambiguous to Node, but some minimal static servers send the wrong MIME type for [38;5;153m.mjs[39m and browsers then refuse the
  module).

  This is the only part of the work that reaches outside the feature, so it's yours to decide rather than mine to
  assume.

